-5484-917b-1e9e4c7a6e2b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153074 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153139 #edb/ref 1061 27256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153139 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "519e6eb2-acac-5f16-b760-eec54f701882" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153139 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153201 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153139 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153201 #edb/ref 1061 37513 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153201 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "be12b013-a739-53f2-9bc3-8d5526146361" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153201 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153361 #edb/ref 1064 "**Complex vectors** are vectors whose entries are *complex numbers*, such as \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1+\\text{i} \\\\ 3+2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n In general, $\\mathbb{C}^n$ is the space of complex vectors with $n$ entries.\n\nComplex vectors work mostly the same as real vectors (we can add them, multiply them by a scalar, etc). However, complex vectors have a few extra properties:\n\n- We can take the complex conjugate of a vector by taking the complex conjugate of each component:\n \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1+\\text{i} \\\\ 3+2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Complex vectors have a real and imaginary part. This means that we can write a vector in the form $\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{\\color{blue}a} + \\textrm{i}\\mathbf{\\color{red}b},$ where ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}} = {\\color{blue}\\textrm{Re}(\\mathbf{x})}$ and ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{b}} = {\\color{red}\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{x})}.$\n\nThe real part of the vector contains the real part of each entry, and the imaginary part of the vector contains the imaginary part of each entry:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1+\\text{i} \\\\ 3+2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153361 #edb/ref 1065 4042 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153361 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0a1bb120-504f-5cb4-978c-b6e7712d5665" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153361 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153423 #edb/ref 1064 "The complex conjugate of vectors has a few useful properties:\n\n- We can distribute the complex conjugate over individual vectors in a sum: \n\n$$\n\\overline{\\mathbf{z}_1 + \\mathbf{z}_2} = \\overline{\\mathbf{z}_1} + \\overline{\\mathbf{z}_2}\n$$\n\n- We can distribute the complex conjugate over the scalar and the corresponding vector:\n\n$$\n\\overline{k \\mathbf{z}} = \\overline{k} \\cdot \\overline{\\mathbf{z}}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153423 #edb/ref 1065 6189 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153423 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ad9cb744-a97a-521e-b9c8-29ae137721bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153423 #edb/ref 1067 "Properties of Vectors Over the Complex Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157303 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050769 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157303 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157303 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157303 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241178 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157303 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157303 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157303 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354344 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157303 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354345 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157303 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157303 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354347 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157303 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354348 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157303 #edb/ref 1039 "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157303 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186157107 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157303 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "61e9c047-0bbf-560d-b803-87703164db5b" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
